'''''Pyracantha coccinea''''', the '''scarlet firethorn'''<ref>{{PLANTS|id=PYCO2|taxon=Pyracantha coccinea|accessdate=16 October 2015}}</ref> is the European species of [[Pyracantha|firethorn]] or '''red firethorn''' that has been cultivated in gardens since the late 16th century.<ref>Alice M. Coats, ''Garden Shrubs and Their Histories'' (1964) 1992, ''s.v.'' "Pyracantha" notes that it does not appear in [[John Gerard]]'s ''Herball'' of 1597 but was in gardens before 1629, when [[John Parkinson (botanist)|John Parkinson]] mentions it, as the "ever greene Hawthorne or prickly Corall tree".</ref> 

==Description==
[[File:Pyracantha coccinea Ognik szkarłatny 2019-05-24 02.jpg|thumb|right|''Pyracantha coccinea'' flowering in the [[University of Wrocław Botanical Garden]]]]
===Vegetative characteristics===
''Pyracantha coccinea'' is an evergreen<ref name = "Baumkunde">Feuerdorn (''Pyracantha coccinea''). (n.d.). Baumkunde. Retrieved August 5, 2026, from https://www.baumkunde.de/Pyracantha_coccinea/</ref> or deciduous,<ref name = "MOBOT">''Pyracantha coccinea''. (n.d.). Missouri Botanical Garden. Retrieved August 5, 2026, from https://plantfinder.mobot.org/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?kempercode=e940</ref> up to 2 m tall shrub<ref name = "InfoFlora">''Pyracantha coccinea'' M. Roem. (n.d.). InfoFlora the National Data and Information Center on the Swiss Flora. Retrieved August 5, 2026, from https://www.infoflora.ch/en/flora/pyracantha-coccinea.html</ref> with stems bearing sharp thorns.<ref name = "NCEGPT" />
===Generative characteristics===
The many-flowered inflorescence<ref name = "Trees and Shrubs Online">''Pyracantha coccinea'' Roem. (n.d.). Trees and Shrubs Online. Retrieved August 5, 2026, from https://www.treesandshrubsonline.org/articles/pyracantha/pyracantha-coccinea/</ref> bears white,<ref name = "NCEGPT" /> bisexual, 7-8 mm wide flowers.<ref name = "Baumkunde" /> The fruit is red, 5 mm wide, globose to egg-shaped.<ref name = "InfoFlora" />
===Cytology===
The chromosome count is 2n = 34.<ref name = "Flora of North America">''Pyracantha coccinea'' in Flora of North America @ efloras.org. (n.d.). Retrieved August 5, 2026, from http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=242417076</ref>

==Taxonomy==
It was described by [[Max Joseph Roemer]] in 1847.<ref name = "IPNI">''Pyracantha coccinea'' M.Roem. (n.d.). International Plant Names Index. Retrieved August 5, 2026, from https://www.ipni.org/n/730611-1</ref>
===Etymology===
The genus name ''Pyracantha'', from the Greek ''pyr'' meaning ''fire'' and ''akantha'' meaning ''thorn'', refers to the thorny branches and the bright red fruit.<ref name = "NCEGPT">NC State University & N.C. A&T State University. (n.d.). ''Pyracantha coccinea''. North Carolina Extension Gardener Plant Toolbox. Retrieved August 5, 2026, from https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/pyracantha-coccinea/</ref> The specific epithet ''coccinea'', from the Latin ''coccineus'', means ''deep red''.<ref>''Echeveria''. (n.d.). International Crassulaceae Network. Retrieved August 5, 2026, from https://www.crassulaceae.ch/de/artikel?akID=48&aaID=2&aID=1129</ref>

==Distribution and habitat== 
It is native to Albania, Bulgaria, France, Greece, Iran, Italy, Crimean Peninsula, Lebanon, Syria, North Caucasus, Balkan Peninsula, Spain, Switzerland, Transcaucasus, and Turkey.<ref name = powo />

==Uses==
In England, since the late 18th century, it has been used to cover unsightly walls.<ref>Coats (1964) 1992.</ref>

== Cultivars ==
* ''Pyracantha coccinea'' 'Kasan'.<ref>[https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/74440/i-Pyracantha-coccinea-i-Kasan/Details ''P. coccinea'' 'Kasan'] at www.rhs.org.uk. Retrieved 30 Aug 2017.</ref>
* ''Pyracantha coccinea'' 'Lalandei'. About 1874, M. Lalande, a nurseryman in [[Angers]], France, selected from seedlings of ''P. coccinea'' an improved form, more freely berrying than the type. A sport has produced a yellow-berried form. These, and further selections, have largely ousted the ordinary form from nursery stock.<ref>Coats (1964) 1992.</ref>
* ''Pyracantha coccinea'' 'Sparkler'.<ref>[https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/132703/i-Pyracantha-coccinea-i-Sparkler/Details ''P. coccinea'' 'Sparkler'] at www.rhs.org.uk. Retrieved 30 Aug 2017.</ref>

